l three possibilities are precisely the non‑sunny directions.  \nConsequently, if a line through two points of \\(T_{3}\\) is sunny, the two points must differ in both coordinates and in their sum.  \nChecking all \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) pairs of points in \\(T_{3}\\) shows that the only sunny pairs are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\qquad \\{(1,3),(2,1)\\},\\qquad \\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\n\\]\n\neach giving a line of slope \\(-\\tfrac12,\\,-2,\\;1\\) respectively.  \nNo third point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on any of these three lines, so a sunny line can never contain three points of \\(T_{3}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nA non‑sunny line can contain at most three points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe three families of non‑sunny lines are:\n\\[\nx=c,\\; y=c,\\; x+y=c .\n\\]\nInspecting \\(T_{3}\\) we see\n\n* \\(x=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\);\n* \\(y=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\);\n* \\(x+y=4\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).\n\nAll other choices of \\(c\\) give at most two points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Main Argument  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\}\\) of three distinct lines covering \\(T_{3}\\) with exactly two of them sunny.  \nLet \\(L_{s}\\) be a non‑sunny line (the third line). By Lemma 2, \\(L_{s}\\) contains at most three points of \\(T_{3}\\).  \nThe two sunny lines together can cover at most \\(2+2=4\\) points (Lemma 1).  \nHence we must have  \n\n\\[\n|T_{3}\\cap L_{s}| \\ge 2,\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\notherwise the two sunny lines would need to cover at least five points, which is impossible.\n\nWe now examine every possible non‑sunny line that satisfies (1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Case 1.** \\(L_{s}\\) is the line \\(x+y=4\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nL_{s}\\cap T_{3}= \\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe remaining points are \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny two of these share either the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate or the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, so any line through two of them is non‑sunny.  \nThus each of the three points would require its own sunny line, contradicting the hypothesis of exactly two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Case 2.** \\(L_{s}\\) is the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nL_{s}\\cap T_{3}= \\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe uncovered points are \\(\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nAgain any two of these share a coordinate, so any line through two of them is non‑sunny.  \nHence three sunny lines would be needed – contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Case 3.** \\(L_{s}\\) is the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \nSymmetric to Case 2:  \n\n\\[\nL_{s}\\cap T_{3}= \\{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\},\n\\]\nleaving \\(\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\), which also forces three sunny lines.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Case 4.** \\(L_{s}\\) is the vertical line \\(x=2\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nL_{s}\\cap T_{3}= \\{(2,1),(2,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe five remaining points are \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nA sunny line can contain at most two of them; the only sunny pair among these is \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \nAfter using a sunny line for that pair, the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) remain, and they lie on the same vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is non‑sunny.  \nThus each would need its own sunny line, requiring three sunny lines in total – contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Case 5.** \\(L_{s}\\) is the horizontal line \\(y=2\\).  \nAnalogous to Case 4:  \n\n\\[\nL_{s}\\cap T_{3}= \\{(1,2),(2,2)\\},\n\\]\nleaving \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nThe only sunny pair among the leftovers is \\((2,1),(1,3)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).  \nThe two poi