            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}= \\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that must lie on at least one of the three lines.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{y}:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{-1}:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\nHence a non‑sunny line belongs to one of the three families  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nAny sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo points of \\(T_{3}\\) determine a line.  \nIf the two points have the same \\(x\\)–coordinate, the line is vertical (\\(x=c\\));  \nif they have the same \\(y\\)–coordinate, the line is horizontal (\\(y=c\\));  \nif they have the same sum \\(x+y\\), the line has slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nAll three possibilities are precisely the non‑sunny directions.  \n\nConsequently a sunny line must join a pair of points that differ in both coordinates and in their sum.  \nChecking all \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) pairs of points in \\(T_{3}\\) yields exactly three such pairs:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1)\\text{ and }(2,2) &&\\text{slope }1,\\\\\n&(1,2)\\text{ and }(3,1) &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\n&(1,3)\\text{ and }(2,1) &&\\text{slope }-2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNo third point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on any of these three lines, so a sunny line can never contain three points of \\(T_{3}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nA non‑sunny line can contain at most three points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe three families of non‑sunny lines are \\(x=c\\), \\(y=c\\) and \\(x+y=c\\).  \nScanning the six points we obtain  \n\n* \\(x=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\);  \n* \\(y=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\);  \n* \\(x+y=4\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).\n\nAll other choices of \\(c\\) give at most two points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Main Argument  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exist three distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\}\\) covering \\(T_{3}\\) with exactly two of them sunny.  \nLet \\(L_{s}\\) be the (unique) non‑sunny line among them.  \nBy Lemma 2, \\(L_{s}\\) is one of the following six lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c}\nx=1,\\; x=2,\\; y=1,\\; y=2,\\; x+y=3,\\; x+y=4 .\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nWe treat each possibility separately and show that the remaining points cannot be covered by only two sunny lines (each of which can cover at most two points by Lemma 1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Case 1.** \\(L_{s}=x+y=4\\).  \n\n\\(L_{s}\\cap T_{3}= \\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nThe uncovered points are \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny two of these share either the same \\(x\\) or the same \\(y\\); therefore any line through two of them is non‑sunny.  \nThus each of the three points would need its own sunny line, contradicting the hypothesis of exactly two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Case 2.** \\(L_{s}=x=1\\).  \n\n\\(L_{s}\\cap T_{3}= \\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\}\\).  \nUncovered: \\(\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nAgain any two share a coordinate, so each would require a separate sunny line – impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Case 3.** \\(L_{s}=y=1\\).  \n\nSymmetric to Case 2; the uncovered set \\(\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\) forces three sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Case 4.** \\(L_{s}=x=2\\).  \n\n\\(L_{s}\\cap T_{3}= \\{(2,1),(2,2)\\}\\).  \nRemaining points: \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nAmong these, the only sunny pair is \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \nUsing a sunny line for that pair leaves \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\), which lie on the same vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny).  \nHence each of them would need a distinct sunny line, again requiring three sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Case 5.** \\(L_{s}=y=2\\).  \n\n\\(L_{s}\\cap T_{3}= \\{(1,2),(2,2)\\}\\).  \nRemaining points: \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nThe only sunny pair among them is \\((2,1),(1,3)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).  \nAfter covering that pair, the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) lie on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (non‑sunny), so each would need its own sunny line – contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Case 6.** \\(L_{s}=x+y=3\\).  \n\n\\(L_{s}\\cap T_{3}= \\{(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nRemaining points: \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nThe only sunny pair among these is \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \nCovering that pair leaves \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), which are on the line \\(x+y=4\\) (slope \\(-1\\), non‑sunny).  \nThus each would need a separate sunny line, again exceeding the allowed two.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Case 7.** \\(L_{s}=x=3\\) or \\(y=3\\).  \n\nEach contains only one point of \\(T_{3}\\); the two sunny lines would then have to cover the remaining five 