s of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\nSince we have three lines to cover six points, each line must contain exactly two points, and the six points must be partitioned into three disjoint pairs, each pair lying on a sunny line.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Choosing three admissible pairs  \n\nConsider the following three pairs:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{1}&:\\;(1,1)\\ \\text{and}\\ (2,2),\\\\\nP_{2}&:\\;(1,2)\\ \\text{and}\\ (3,1),\\\\\nP_{3}&:\\;(1,3)\\ \\text{and}\\ (2,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe slopes of the lines through each pair are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{1}&=\\frac{2-1}{2-1}=1,\\\\[2mm]\nm_{2}&=\\frac{1-2}{3-1}=-\\frac12,\\\\[2mm]\nm_{3}&=\\frac{1-3}{2-1}=-2,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nnone of which equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), or \\(-1\\). Hence the three lines determined by the pairs are sunny and have distinct slopes, so they are distinct lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Equations of the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\ y=x \\qquad\\text{(through }(1,1),(2,2)),\\\\[2mm]\nL_{2}&:\\ y-2=-\\frac12\\,(x-1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52 \n      \\qquad\\text{(through }(1,2),(3,1)),\\\\[2mm]\nL_{3}&:\\ y-3=-2\\,(x-1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; y=-2x+5 \n      \\qquad\\text{(through }(1,3),(2,1)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three lines are sunny because their slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Verification that every point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on a line  \n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Lies on which line? | Check |\n|-----------------|---------------------|-------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) | \\(1 = 1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) | \\(2 = 2\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(2 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot1 + \\tfrac52 = 2\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(1 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot3 + \\tfrac52 = 1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) | \\(3 = -2\\cdot1 +5 = 3\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) | \\(1 = -2\\cdot2 +5 = 1\\) \u2713 |\n\nEvery required point is covered.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited three distinct sunny lines that together contain all points of \\(T_{3}\\). Therefore, for \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=3\\) (i.e. all three lines sunny) is attainable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3,\\;k=3\\text{ is possible.}}\n\\]"
