            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le 4\\}\n      =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must exhibit three distinct lines, each *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)), such that every point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n---\n\n### 1.  A sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\)\n\nIf a line is sunny, its slope \\(m\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nm\\neq0,\\qquad m\\neq\\infty,\\qquad m\\neq-1 .\n\\]\n\nAmong the six points of \\(T_{3}\\) any three that are collinear would have to lie on one of the following forbidden lines:\n\n* vertical line \\(x=1\\) (slope \\(\\infty\\)) containing \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\);\n* horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (slope \\(0\\)) containing \\((2,1),(3,1)\\);\n* line \\(x+y=2\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) containing \\((1,1),(2,0)\\) – not in \\(T_{3}\\);\n* line \\(x+y=3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) containing \\((1,2),(2,1)\\);\n* line \\(x+y=4\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) containing \\((1,3),(2,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\).\n\nA direct inspection shows that the only triples of points from \\(T_{3}\\) that are collinear have slopes \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). Hence a line whose slope avoids these three values can meet \\(T_{3}\\) in **at most two** points.\n\nSince we have three lines to cover six points, each line must contain exactly two points, and the six points must be partitioned into three disjoint pairs, each pair lying on a sunny line.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Selecting three admissible pairs\n\nConsider the following three pairs:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{1}&:\\;(1,1)\\ \\text{and}\\ (2,2),\\\\\nP_{2}&:\\;(1,2)\\ \\text{and}\\ (3,1),\\\\\nP_{3}&:\\;(1,3)\\ \\text{and}\\ (2,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe slopes of the lines through each pair are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{1}&=\\frac{2-1}{2-1}=1,\\\\[2mm]\nm_{2}&=\\frac{1-2}{3-1}=-\\frac12,\\\\[2mm]\nm_{3}&=\\frac{1-3}{2-1}=-2,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nnone of which equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), or \\(-1\\). Therefore the three lines determined by the pairs are sunny and have distinct slopes, so they are distinct lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Equations of the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\ y=x \\qquad\\text{(through }(1,1),(2,2)),\\\\[2mm]\nL_{2}&:\\ y-2=-\\frac12\\,(x-1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52 \n      \\qquad\\text{(through }(1,2),(3,1)),\\\\[2mm]\nL_{3}&:\\ y-3=-2\\,(x-1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; y=-2x+5 \n      \\qquad\\text{(through }(1,3),(2,1)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three lines are sunny because their slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Verification that every point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on a line  \n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Lies on which line? | Check |\n|-----------------|---------------------|-------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) | \\(1 = 1\\) ✓ |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) | \\(2 = 2\\) ✓ |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(2 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot1 + \\tfrac52 = 2\\) ✓ |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(1 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot3 + \\tfrac52 = 1\\) ✓ |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) | \\(3 = -2\\cdot1 +5 = 3\\) ✓ |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) | \\(1 = -2\\cdot2 +5 = 1\\) ✓ |\n\nEvery required point is covered.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited three distinct sunny lines that together contain all point