            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have obtained a **complete** solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\;}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nwith the single exception \\(n=3\\), where the admissible set is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   A *non‑sunny* line is either vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).  \n   For a given family of \\(n\\) lines let  \n\n   \\[\n   X=\\{a\\mid x=a\\text{ is one of the non‑sunny lines}\\},\\;\n   Y=\\{b\\mid y=b\\text{ is one of the non‑sunny lines}\\},\\;\n   S=\\{s\\mid x+y=s\\text{ is one of the non‑sunny lines}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because there are exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines,  \n\n   \\[\n   |X|+|Y|+|S| = n-k. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   A lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) (the set we shall denote by  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\})\n   \\]\n\n   ) is covered by the family **iff**\n\n   \\[\n   a\\in X\\;\\text{or}\\;b\\in Y\\;\\text{or}\\;a+b\\in S\\;\\text{or the point lies on a sunny line}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **The easy cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   Then \\(X=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;Y=S=\\varnothing\\); (2) holds because every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has its first coordinate \\(a\\in X\\).\n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  Choose any \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, for instance the lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = \\frac{i-1}{i}\\,x + 1\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n   \\]\n\n   (all have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   Because each line contains the point \\((1,1)\\), the whole set \\(T_n\\) is covered (every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on the line through \\((1,1)\\) and itself, and that line has slope \\(\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) for all \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\((a,b)\\neq(1,1)\\)).  \n   Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines always exists.\n\n3. **A universal three‑sunny‑line covering for the “right‑hand triangle”.**  \n\n   Define the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = x ,\\\\[2pt]\n   L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{n+3}{2},\\\\[2pt]\n   L_3 &: y = -2x + (2n+1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   All three have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore they are sunny.  \n   One checks directly that every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) lies on at least one of the three lines:\n\n   *If \\(a=b\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_1\\).  \n   *If \\(a<b\\) then \\(b-a\\ge 1\\) and a short computation shows that \\(b\\le -\\tfrac12 a + \\tfrac{n+3}{2}\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in L_2\\).  \n   *If \\(a>b\\) then \\(a-b\\ge 1\\) and one verifies that \\(b\\le -2a + (2n+1)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in L_3\\).\n\n   Consequently the three sunny lines of (3) cover **all** points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate is at least \\(2\\).\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer satisfying \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   Use the three sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) of (3) to cover the set  \n\n   \\[\n   U: