\\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=1,2\\) when \\(n\\ge 4\\).**  \n\n   Assume a configuration with \\(k\\le 2\\) sunny lines exists for some \\(n\\ge4\\).  \n   Let \\(X,Y,S\\) be defined as in §1. From (1) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |X|+|Y|+|S| = n-k \\ge n-2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently each of the three complements  \n\n   \\[\n   X^{c}=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus X,\\qquad\n   Y^{c}=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus Y,\\qquad\n   S^{c}=\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus S\n   \\]\n\n   has size at most \\(2\\).  \n\n   Choose two distinct elements \\(a_1,a_2\\in X^{c}\\) (possible because \\(|X^{c}|\\ge2\\)) and two distinct elements \\(b_1,b_2\\in Y^{c}\\).  \n   Consider the four sums \\(s_{ij}=a_i+b_j\\;(i,j\\in\\{1,2\\})\\).  \n   All four lie in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\).  \n\n   Because \\(|S^{c}|\\le2\\), at most two of the four numbers can belong to \\(S^{c}\\); therefore **at least one** of the four sums belongs to \\(S\\).  \n   Without loss of generality suppose \\(s_{11}=a_1+b_1\\in S\\).  \n\n   The three remaining sums \\(s_{12},s_{21},s_{22}\\) are not in \\(S\\); i.e. they belong to \\(S^{c}\\).  \n   For each of these three pairs \\((a_i,b_j)\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   a_i\\notin X,\\qquad b_j\\notin Y,\\qquad a_i+b_j\\notin S,\n   \\]\n\n   which contradicts the covering condition (2) because the corresponding lattice point \\((a_i,b_j)\\) would be uncovered by any line (the only possible covering lines are the two sunny ones, but a sunny line cannot contain two of the three points simultaneously – a direct computation shows that any two of the three points have either equal \\(a\\), equal \\(b\\) or equal sum, all of which have already been excluded).  \n\n   Hence a configuration with \\(k\\le2\\) sunny lines cannot exist when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   * For \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\).  \n   * For every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible