(-1\\) lines, respectively. Equation (1) follows directly from the fact that each non‑sunny line contributes exactly one element to exactly one of the three sets, and there are \\(n-k\\) such lines. Condition (2) is a precise reformulation of the covering requirement.  \n**Conclusion:** Correct. No issue.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2 – “Easy” cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\n#### (a) \\(k=0\\)\n\n> “Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\). … then every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has its first coordinate \\(a\\in X\\).”\n\n**Verification:** The vertical lines are non‑sunny, distinct, and there are exactly \\(n\\) of them. Since each point of \\(T_n\\) has \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), it lies on the line \\(x=a\\). Hence the covering holds and \\(k=0\\) is feasible.  \n**Conclusion:** Correct.\n\n#### (b) \\(k=n\\)\n\n> “Choose any \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, for instance the lines  \n> \\[\n> L_i:\\; y = \\frac{i-1}{i}\\,x + 1\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n> \\]  \n> (all have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)). Because each line contains the point \\((1,1)\\), the whole set \\(T_n\\) is covered (every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on the line through \\((1,1)\\) and itself, and that line has slope \\(\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) for all \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\((a,b)\\neq(1,1)\\)).”\n\n**Verification of the claim “all have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)”:**  \n- For \\(i=1\\) the slope is \\((i-1)/i = 0\\); the line \\(y=1\\) is horizontal, **not sunny**.  \n- For \\(i>1\\) the slopes are positive fractions \\(<1\\), which are indeed different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n\nThus the statement that **all** \\(L_i\\) are sunny is false.\n\n**Verification of the claim “each line contains the point \\((1,1)\\)”:**  \n- Substituting \\((1,1)\\) into \\(y = \\frac{i-1}{i}x + 1\\) gives \\(1 = \\frac{i-1}{i}\\cdot 1 + 1 = \\frac{i-1+i}{i}= \\frac{2i-1}{i}\\), which holds only for \\(i=1\\). For \\(i\\ge2\\) the equality fails, so those lines do **not** pass through \\((1,1)\\).\n\n**Consequences:** The proposed family does **not** consist of \\(n\\) sunny lines, and it does **not** guarantee that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of the lines. Hence the construction for \\(k=n\\) is invalid.  \n**Issue:** **Critical Error** (as listed in the Summary).\n\n---\n\n### Step 3 – “Universal three‑sunny‑line covering”\n\n> “Define the three sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> L_1 : y = x ,\\qquad\n> L_2 : y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{n+3}{2},\\qquad\n> L_3 : y = -2x + (2n+1).\n> \\]  \n> … One checks directly that every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) lies on at least one of the three lines:  \n> *If \\(a=b\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_1\\).  \n> *If \\(a<b\\) then \\(b-a\\ge 1\\) and a short computation shows that \\(b\\le -\\tfrac12 a + \\tfrac{n+3}{2}\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in L_2\\).  \n> *If \\(a>b\\) then \\(a-b\\ge 1\\) and one verifies that \\(b\\le -2a + (2n+1)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in L_3\\).”\n\n**Verification of the logical step “\\(b\\le -\\tfrac12 a + \\frac{n+3}{2}\\) ⇒ \\((a,b)\\in L_2\\)”:**  \nA point lies **on** the line \\(L_2\\) precisely when equality holds: \\(b = -\\tfrac12 a + \\frac{n+3}{2}\\). The inequality \\(b\\le\\) merely places the point in a half‑plane that contains the line, not on the line itself. For many admissible points (e.g. \\(n=5,\\;a=2,\\;b=4\\)) the inequality holds but the point is not on \\(L_2\\). The same flaw occurs for the claim concerning \\(L_3\\). Consequently the statement that **all** points with \\(a\\ge2\\) are covered by the three lines is false.  \n**Issue:** **Critical Error** (as listed in the Summary).\n\n---\n\n### Step 4 – Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\)\n\n> “Use the three sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) … to cover the set \\(U:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge 2\\}\\).  \n> The remaining points are exactly those of the first column, \\(C:=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).  \n> Cover the column \\(C\\) with the \\(k-3\\) additional sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> M_j:\\; y = \\frac{j}{j+1}\\,x + 1\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,k-3).\n> \\]  \n> Each line \\(M_j\\) passes through the point \\((1,1)\\) and through the distinct point \\((1,1+j)\\); therefore … the family … consists of exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines and covers every point of \\(C\\) (the point \\((1,1+j)\\) lies on \\(M_j\\)).”\n\n**Verification of the claim “\\(M_j\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,1+j)\\)”:**  \n- Substituting \\(x=1\\) into \\(y = \\frac{j}{j+1}x + 1\\) yields \\(y = \\frac{j}{j+1}+1 = \\frac{2j+1}{j+1}\\), which equals \\(1\\) only when \\(j=0\\) (not allowed) and equals \\(1+j\\) only when \\(j=0\\) as well. Hence for any admissible \\(j\\ge1\\) the line does **not** pass through either \\((1,1)\\) or \\((1,1+j)\\).  \n\nThus the column \\(C\\) is **not** covered by the proposed \\(M_j\\) lines. Moreover, the construction relies on the false covering property of the three lines from Step 3, so the entire argument for \\(k\\ge3\\) collapses.  \n**Issue:** **Critical Error** (as listed in the Summary).\n\n---\n\n### Step 5 – The case \\(n=3\\)\n\n#### (a) \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n> “\\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\) …  \n> \\(k=1\\): the family \\(\\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=x\\}\\) …”\n\n**Verification:** Both families consist of three distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(y=x\\)) is sunny, and a direct check shows that every point of \\(T_3\\) is covered. These constructions are correct.\n\n#### (b) \\(k=3\\)\n\n> “the three sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> y=x,\\qquad\n> y=-\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n> y=-2x+5\n> \\]  \n> are distinct, sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_3\\).”\n\n**Verification:** As shown in the analysis, each of the six points of \\(T_3\\) lies on one of these three lines, and none of the lines has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). Hence the construction is valid.\n\n#### (c) Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\n> “No configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists: … a direct case‑by‑case inspection shows that in each case at least one point of \\(T_3\\) remains uncovered.”\n\n**Verification:** The statement is plausible, but the solution does **not** present the exhaustive case analysis. The absence of a concrete argument leaves a gap in rigor.  \n**Issue:** **Justification Gap** (as listed in the Summary).\n\n---\n\n### Step 6 – Impossibility of \\(k=1,2\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n> “Assume a configuration with \\(k\\le2\\) sunny lines exists … From (1) we have \\(|X|+|Y|+|S| = n-k \\ge n-2\\). Consequently each of the three complements  \n> \\[\n> X^{c}=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus X,\\qquad\n> Y^{c}=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus Y,\\qquad\n> S^{c}=\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus S\n> \\]  \n> has size at most \\(2\\). …”\n\n**Verification of the size‑bound claim:**  \nFrom \\(|X|+|Y|+|S| \\ge n-2\\) we can only infer that the *sum* of the three complement sizes is at most \\(2n+2\\); there is no reason why each individual complement must be \\(\\le2\\). For example, with \\(n=5\\) one could have \\(|X|=3,\\;|Y|=0,\\;|S|=0\\), giving \\(|X^{c}|=2\\) but \\(|Y^{c}|=5\\) and \\(|S^{c}|=5\\). Hence the assertion that all three complements have size at most 2 is **false**.\n\nBecause the subsequent selection of two elements from \\(X^{c}\\) and two from \\(Y^{c}\\) and the counting argument about the four sums rely on this false bound, the whole contradiction collapses.  \n**Issue:** **Critical Error** (as listed in the Summary).\n\n---\n\n### Step 7 – Conclusion\n\n> “For \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\).  \n> For every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0\\) or \\(k\\in\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\).”\n\n**Verification:** The conclusion is drawn from the constructions and impossibility arguments examined above. Since the constructions for \\(k=n\\) and for all \\(k\\ge3\\) (Steps 2‑4) are invalid, and the impossibility proof for \\(k\\le2\\) (Step 6) is also invalid, the conclusion is **unsupported**.  \n**Issue:** The conclusion inherits the **Critical Errors** identified earlier.\n\n--- \n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution contains several fundamental mistakes: the proposed families of sunny lines for \\(k=n\\) and for the universal three‑line covering are incorrect; the construction for arbitrary \\(k\\ge3\\) is based on those false statements; the impossibility argument for \\(k\\le2\\) misuses the size of complements; and the claim that \\(k=2\\) is impossible for \\(n=3\\) lacks a rigorous justification. Consequently the answer is not proven and the solution is invalid."
