\\,},\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   which passes through the two points \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).  \n   The vertical lines cover all points whose first coordinate is at most\n   \\(n-1\\); the only point not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\), and it lies on\n   \\(L\\).  The slope of \\(L\\) is \\(-\\frac1{n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence\n   \\(L\\) is sunny.\n\n3. **A universal three‑sunny‑line covering.**  \n\n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = x,\\\\[2pt]\n   L_2 &: y = -\\frac12\\,x+\\frac{n+3}{2},\\\\[2pt]\n   L_3 &: y = -2\\,x+(2n+1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   All three slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the\n   lines are sunny.\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\).  \n   Exactly one of the following three mutually exclusive possibilities\n   holds:\n\n   * **(i) \\(b=a\\).**  Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_1\\).\n\n   * **(ii) \\(b>a\\).**  Write \\(b=a+r\\) with \\(r\\ge1\\).  \n     Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n     \\[\n     a+r\\le n+1-a\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n     r\\le\\frac{n+3}{2}-\\frac a2 .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence  \n\n     \\[\n     b = a+r\\le -\\frac12 a+\\frac{n+3}{2},\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. the point satisfies the equation of \\(L_2\\); consequently\n     \\((a,b)\\in L_2\\).\n\n   * **(iii) \\(b<a\\).**  Write \\(a=b+s\\) with \\(s\\ge1\\).  \n     From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     b+s+b\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n     b\\le -2a+(2n+1),\n     \\]\n\n     which is precisely the equation of \\(L_3\\); thus \\((a,b)\\in L_3\\).\n\n   In every case the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the three\n   lines.  Consequently the three sunny lines of (2) cover the whole set  \n\n   \\[\n   U:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge2\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   *Use the three lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) of (2) to cover the set \\(U\