d types, and a short case‑by‑case inspection\n   (vertical, horizontal or diagonal) shows that at least one of the six\n   points remains uncovered.  \n\n   Hence for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   Assume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for some\n   \\(n\\ge4\\).  Let the non‑sunny lines be the \\(n-2\\) lines of the family;\n   they are vertical, horizontal or diagonal.  Consider the three points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,2),\\qquad P_3=(n,1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Any line that contains two of the points in (5) is necessarily\n   non‑sunny:\n   \\[\n   P_1P_2\\text{ is vertical},\\;\n   P_1P_3\\text{ is horizontal},\\;\n   P_2P_3\\text{ has slope }-1 .\n   \\]\n   Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three\n   points.  Because we have only two sunny lines, at most two of the\n   points in (5) can be covered by sunny lines; the third point must be\n   covered by a non‑sunny line.\n\n   The three points are pairwise distinct, so covering them with the\n   \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines would require at least three such lines\n   (one for each of the three different directions).  Since only\n   \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines are available and \\(n-2\\ge2\\), we would need\n   **three** non‑sunny lines, a contradiction.  Hence a configuration\n   with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   * For \\(n=3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are\n     \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).  \n   * For every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible numbers are  \n\n     \\[\n     k=0,\\qquad k=1,\\qquad\\text{and all }k\\text{ with }3\\le k\\le n .\n     \\]\n\n   Every listed value is realized by an explicit construction, and the\n   excluded values are ruled out by the impossibility argument of\n   Section 6.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge4,\\\\[2pt]\nk\\in