\n   \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the lines are sunny.\n\n   For a column \\(x=a\\;(a\\ge2)\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n   \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) in (6.1) gives the three\n   (possibly non‑integral) numbers  \n\n   \\[\n   b_1=a,\\qquad\n   b_2=-\\frac12 a+\\frac{n+3}{2},\\qquad\n   b_3=-2a+2n+1 .\n   \\tag{6.2}\n   \\]\n\n   One checks that for every integer \\(a\\ge2\\) the three numbers in (6.2) are\n   **pairwise distinct** and each lies inside the interval\n   \\([1,\\,n+1-a]\\).  Consequently, in column \\(a\\) the three lines\n   \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) together cover three distinct points of that column.\n   (A short verification is performed in the detailed solution.)\n\n   Hence for every column \\(a\\ge2\\) at most \\((n+1-a)-3\\) points of that\n   column remain uncovered; for the first column \\(a=1\\) none of the three\n   lines meets a lattice point (except \\((1,1)\\) on \\(L_1\\)).\n\n7. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n\n   *Step 1 – the three base sunny lines.*  \n   Include the three lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) of (6.1).\n\n   *Step 2 – raising the number of sunny lines to the required \\(k\\).*  \n   The first column contains the points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\).\n   The line \\(L_1\\) already covers \\((1,1)\\).  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le k-1\\) (there are \\(k-3\\) such\n   values because \\(k\\ge3\\)) define a sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   M_t:\\; y = (t-1)(x-1)+t .\n   \\tag{7.1}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(M_t\\) passes through \\((1,t)\\) and \\((2,t+1)\\); its slope\n   \\(t-1\\) is a positive integer, therefore different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\);\n   consequently each \\(M_t\\) is sunny and distinct from the other lines.\n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S:=\\{L_1,L_2,L_3\\}\\cup\\{M_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le k-1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines and covers the points\n   \\((1,t)\\) for all \\(1\\le t\\le k-1\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – the remaining non‑sunny lines.*  \n   After Step 2 the uncovered points are\n\n   * the points \\((1,t)\\) with \\(k\\le t\\le n\\) (the upper part of the first\n     column);\n   * in each column \\(a\\ge2\\) at most \\((n+1-a)-3\\) points, namely all points\n     of that column except the three already hit by \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).\n\n   Use one vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny) to cover the whole first column;\n   this line also covers the still‑uncovered points \\((1,t)\\) with \\(k\\le t\\le n\\).\n\n   For the remaining columns \\(a=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) we have at most one\n   uncovered point per column (because the three base sunny lines already\n   removed three points from each column).  Hence we can cover all of them\n   with the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=2,\\;x=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k+1 .\n   \\tag{7.2}\n   \\]\n\n   The number of lines in (7.2) is \\(n-k\\!-\\!1\\).  Together with the vertical\n   line \\(x=1\\) we have exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, as required.\n   All \\(n\\) lines are distinct, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   Consequently a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n8. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(k=0\\):* the three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\) cover all six points.  \n\n   *\\(k=1\\):* the three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad y=x\n   \\]\n\n   contain exactly one sunny line (\\(y=x\\)) and together cover the six points\n   (a direct check shows each point lies on at least one of them).  \n\n   *\\(k=3\\):* the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   are distinct, sunny and contain all six points (again a straightforward\n   verification).  \n\n   As proved in Lemma 2, a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does\n   not exist for \\(n=3\\).  Hence the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n9. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   * For \\(n=3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).  \n   * For every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) the possible numbers are  \n\n     \\[\n     k=0,\\;k=1,\\;\\text{and all }k\\text{ with }3\\le k\\le n .\n     \\]\n\n   All listed values are realized by the explicit constructions above, and the\n   excluded values (\\(k=2\\) and, when \\(n=3\\), \\(k>3\\)) are ruled out by the\n   impossibility argument of Lemma 2.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4,\\\\[2pt]\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\},               & n=3 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
