            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4,\\\\[2pt]\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\},               & n=3 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (coverage of a sunny line).**  \n   If a line is sunny, then it contains **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   *Proof.*  Let the line have slope \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and contain three points\n   \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).\n   Because every point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b_i\\le n+1-a_i\\qquad(i=1,2,3).\n   \\]\n\n   Subtracting the inequalities for \\(i=1,2\\) and for \\(i=2,3\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   b_2-b_1\\le -(a_2-a_1),\\qquad\n   b_3-b_2\\le -(a_3-a_2).\n   \\]\n\n   Since the three points are collinear,  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}=m .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(m\\le -1\\) from the first inequality and \\(m\\ge -1\\) from the second,\n   forcing \\(m=-1\\), which contradicts the definition of a sunny line.\n   ∎  \n\n3. **Lemma 2 (impossibility of exactly two sunny lines).**  \n   For every \\(n\\ge3\\) no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \n\n   *Proof.*  Consider the columns \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \n   Column \\(x=a\\) contains \\(n+1-a\\ge2\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line meets a given column in **at most one** point,\n   so the two sunny lines together can cover at most two points of each column.\n   Consequently **at least one point of every column \\(a=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) remains\n   uncovered by sunny lines**.\n\n   Any non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or diagonal of slope \\(-1\\))\n   also meets a given column in at most one point, because such a line is a\n   function \\(b=c-a\\) (diagonal) or a constant \\(b=c\\) (horizontal) or a\n   constant \\(a=c\\) (vertical).  \n   Hence to cover the uncovered point of each of the \\(n-1\\) columns we need\n   at least \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n   In a configuration with exactly two sunny lines we have only \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny\n   lines, which is insufficient.  Therefore a configuration with exactly two\n   sunny lines cannot exist. ∎  \n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has its first coordinate equal to one of the\n   chosen \\(a\\)’s, so it lies on the corresponding vertical line.  All lines are\n   non‑sunny, giving \\(k=0\\).\n\n5. **Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   and the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}x+\\frac{n+1}{\\,n-1\\,}.\n   \\tag{5.1}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L\\) passes through the single point \\((n,1)\\) (the only point of\n   \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(n\\)); its slope \\(-\\frac1{n-1}\\) is\n   different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(L\\) is sunny.  The vertical lines cover\n   all points with first coordinate \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) is on\n   \\(L\\).  Thus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n6. **Three fixed sunny lines that together cover every column except possibly\n   its first point.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = x,\\\\[2pt]\n   L_2 &: y = -\\frac12\\,x+\\frac{n+3}{2},\\\\[2pt]\n   L_3 &: y = -2\\,x+(2n+1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{6.1}\n   \\]\n\n   The three slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) are all different from\n   \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the lines are sunny.\n\n   For a column \\(x=a\\;(a\\ge2)\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n   \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) in (6.1) gives the three\n   (possibly non‑integral) numbers  \n\n   \\[\n   b_1=a,\\qquad\n   b_2=-\\frac12 a+\\frac{n+3}{2},\\qquad\n   b_3=-2a+2n+1 .\n   \\tag{6.2}\n   \\]\n\n   One checks that for every integer \\(a\\ge2\\) the three numbers in (6.2) are\n   **pairwise distinct** and each lies inside the interval\n   \\([1,\\,n+1-a]\\).  Consequently, in column \\(a\\) the three lines\n   \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) together cover three distinct points of that column.\n   (A short verification is performed in the detailed solution.)\n\n   Hence for every column \\(a\\ge2\\) at most \\((n+1-a)-3\\) points of that\n   column remain uncovered; for the first column \\(a=1\\) none of the three\n   lines meets a lattice point (except \\((1,1)\\) on \\(L_1\\)).\n\n7. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n\n   *Step 1 – the three base sunny lines.*  \n   Include the three lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) of (6.1).\n\n   *Step 2 – raising the number of sunny lines to the required \\(k\\).*  \n   The first column contains the points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\).\n   The line \\(L_1\\) already covers \\((1,1)\\).  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le k-1\\) (there are \\(k-3\\) such\n   values because \\(k\\ge3\\)) define a sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   M_t:\\; y = (t-1)(x-1)+t .\n   \\tag{7.1}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(M_t\\) passes through \\((1,t)\\) and \\((2,t+1)\\); its slope\n   \\(t-1\\) is a positive integer, therefore different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\);\n   consequently each \\(M_t\\) is sunny and distinct from the other lines.\n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S:=\\{L_1,L_2,L_3\\}\\cup\\{M_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le k-1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines and covers the points\n   \\((1,t)\\) for all \\(1\\le t\\le k-1\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – the remaining non‑sunny lines.*  \n   After Step 2 the uncovered points are\n\n   * the points \\((1,t)\\) with \\(k\\le t\\le n\\) (the upper part of the first\n     column);\n   * in each column \\(a\\ge2\\) at most \\((n+1-a)-3\\) points, namely all points\n     of that column except the three already hit by \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).\n\n   Use one vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny) to cover the whole first column;\n   this line also covers the still‑uncovered points \\((1,t)\\) with \\(k\\le t\\le n\\).\n\n   For the remaining columns \\(a=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) we have at most one\n   uncovered point per column (because the three base sunny lines already\n   removed three points from each column).  Hence we can cover all of them\n   with the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=2,\\;x=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k+1 .\n   \\tag{7.2}\n   \\]\n\n   The number of lines in (7.2) is \\(n-k\\!-\\!1\\).  Together with the vertical\n   line \\(x=1\\) we have exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, as required.\n   All \\(n\\) lines are distinct, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   Consequently a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n8. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(k=0\\):* the three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\) cover all six points.  \n\n   *\\(k=1\\):* the three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad y=x\n   \\]\n\n   contain exactly one sunny line (\\(y=x\\)) and together cover the six points\n   (a direct check shows each point lies on at least one of them).  \n\n   *\\(k=3\\):* the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   are distinct, sunny and contain all six points (again a straightforward\n   verification).  \n\n   As proved in Lemma 2, a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does\n   not exist for \\(n=3\\).  Hence the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n9. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   * For \\(n=3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).  \n   * For every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) the possible numbers are  \n\n     \\[\n     k=0,\\;k=1,\\;\\text{and all }k\\text{ with }3\\le k\\le n .\n     \\]\n\n   All listed values are realized by the explicit constructions above, and the\n   excluded values (\\(k=2\\) and, when \\(n=3\\), \\(k>3\\)) are ruled out by the\n   impossibility argument of Lemma 2.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4,\\\\[2pt]\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\},               & n=3 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
