\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;2k\\ge k+1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;k\\ge1,\n   \\]\n\n   which is true, so the counting argument alone does not forbid large \\(k\\).  \n   The additional restriction comes from the **integrality** of the points on sunny lines.  \n   Because a sunny line has slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\), the set of lattice points it can contain is an arithmetic progression with common difference \\(|m|\\) (if \\(m\\) is rational) or is empty (if \\(m\\) is irrational).  \n   In the triangular region \\(T_n\\) the maximal size of such a progression is **at most three** points; a direct inspection shows that a sunny line can never contain four distinct points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   (Indeed, if a sunny line contained four points \\((a_i,b_i)\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3<a_4\\), the equal slopes between successive pairs would force the slope to be \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of sunny.)  \n\n   Consequently each sunny line can cover **at most three** points of the uncovered triangular set.  \n   With \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines the total number of points they can cover is at most \\(3k\\).  \n   But the uncovered set contains \\(\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\) points, and for \\(k\\ge4\\)\n\n   \\[\n   3k<\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the uncovered points cannot be covered, a contradiction.  \n   Therefore **no configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists**.\n\n5. **Construction for the admissible values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\).  Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has its first coordinate equal to one of the chosen \\(a\\)’s, so it lies on the corresponding vertical line.  All lines are non‑sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y = x-n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L\\) has slope \\(1\\) (hence sunny) and passes through the unique point \\((n,1)\\) of the last column.  All other 