2f1 (that all non\u2011sunny lines are vertical) and also assumes that the only uncovered columns are the two right\u2011most columns. Since Lemma\u202f1 is false, we cannot guarantee that a configuration with two sunny lines must have exactly those uncovered columns. A configuration could use a non\u2011vertical non\u2011sunny line (horizontal or slope\u202f\u20131) to cover points in the right\u2011most columns, invalidating the \u201ctwo largest columns are uncovered\u201d claim.\n\n- Because the foundational reduction is invalid, the entire impossibility argument for \\(k=2\\) collapses. No rigorous proof of impossibility for \\(k=2\\) is provided.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f4 \u2013 Impossibility of\u202f\\(k\\ge4\\)\n\n> \u201cAfter Lemma\u202f1 the uncovered columns are the last \\(k\\) ones\u2026 Claim: A sunny line can contain at most \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) points of this triangle\u2026 Consequently the total number of points that the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover is at most \\(k\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\)\u2026 inequality (3) fails for even \\(k\\) and forces every sunny line to meet every column for odd \\(k\\ge5\\)\u2026 Hence for odd \\(k\\ge5\\) the required covering is impossible. Consequently no configuration exists for any \\(k\\ge4\\).\u201d\n\n**Issues Identified \u2013 Critical Errors**\n\n1. **Dependence on Lemma\u202f1:**  \n   The whole setup (uncovered columns being the last \\(k\\) columns) presumes that all non\u2011sunny lines are vertical, which, as shown, is not guaranteed.\n\n2. **Unproved Claim about Sunny\u2011Line Capacity:**  \n   The statement \u201ca sunny line can contain at most \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) points of this triangle\u201d is asserted without a rigorous proof. The argument given (\u201cif \\(m\\ge0\\)\u2026\u201d) is incomplete and does not cover all possible slopes (e.g., rational slopes with denominator >1). No exhaustive case analysis is provided, so the bound is not established.\n\n3. **Logical Gap in Inequality (3):**  \