\n\n> \u201cLet the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines be \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\). Define three sunny lines  \n> \\(\\ell_{1}: y = -\\tfrac12\\,x + \\tfrac{n+2}{2},\\)  \n> \\(\\ell_{2}: y = -2x + 2n+1,\\)  \n> \\(\\ell_{3}: y =  x-(n-3).\\)  \n> \u2026 (table of coverage) \u2026\u201d\n\n**Issue Identified \u2013 Critical Error**\n\n- **Incorrect line \\(\\ell_{2}\\):**  \n  The intended points to be covered are \\((n-2,3)\\) and \\((n-1,1)\\). The line given in the solution, \\(\\ell_{2}: y = -2x + 2n+1\\), yields  \n  \\[\n  y(n-2) = -2(n-2) + 2n + 1 = 5,\\qquad\n  y(n-1) = -2(n-1) + 2n + 1 = 3,\n  \\]\n  which are **not** the required \\(y\\)-coordinates \\(3\\) and \\(1\\). The correct line through \\((n-2,3)\\) and \\((n-1,1)\\) is \\(y = -2x + 2n - 1\\). As written, \\(\\ell_{2}\\) fails to cover the points it is supposed to, leaving uncovered points in the triangle.\n\n- **Consequences:**  \n  Because \\(\\ell_{2}\\) does not cover the designated points, the claimed covering of all points of \\(T_{n}\\) is false. The existence proof for \\(k=3\\) therefore fails.\n\n- **Note on \\(\\ell_{3}\\):**  \n  The solution initially gave an incorrect \\(\\ell_{3}\\) (slope\u202f1 but intercept \\(n-2\\)), recognized the mistake, and replaced it with \\(\\ell_{3}: y = x-(n-3)\\). This correction is valid, but the remaining error in \\(\\ell_{2}\\) still invalidates the construction.\n\nThus the construction for \\(k=3\\) is incorrect.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f6 \u2013 Conclusion  \n\n> \u201cExistence has been proved for \\(k=0,1,3\\). Non\u2011existence has been proved for every other non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\). Hence the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe conclusion relies on the impossibility arguments for \\(k=2\\) and \\(k\\ge4\\) (both invalid) and on the existence construction for \\(k=3\\) (also invalid). Consequently the conclusion is **not justified** by the presented reasoning.\n\n--- \n\n**