\\\\\n        & (n-2,2) & \\ell_{1}\\\\\n        & (n-2,3) & \\ell_{2}\\\\ \\hline\n   n-1 & (n-1,1) & \\ell_{2}\\\\\n        & (n-1,2) & \\ell_{3}\\\\ \\hline\n   n   & (n,1)   & \\ell_{1}\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n\n   - For \\(\\ell_{1}\\): \\(y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{n+2}{2}\\).  \n     Substituting \\(x=n\\) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=n-2\\) gives \\(y=2\\). Hence \\(\\ell_{1}\\) contains \\((n,1)\\) and \\((n-2,2)\\).\n\n   - For \\(\\ell_{2}\\): \\(y = -2x + 2n - 1\\).  \n     Substituting \\(x=n-1\\) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=n-2\\) gives \\(y=3\\). Hence \\(\\ell_{2}\\) contains \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-2,3)\\).\n\n   - For \\(\\ell_{3}\\): \\(y = x-(n-3)\\).  \n     Substituting \\(x=n-2\\) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=n-1\\) gives \\(y=2\\). Hence \\(\\ell_{3}\\) contains \\((n-2,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\).\n\n   Every point of the three uncovered columns appears in the table, so all points of \\(T_{n}\\) are covered. Together with the vertical lines (6) we have exactly three sunny lines.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   We have shown that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\) exists **iff** the number of sunny lines equals \\(0\\), \\(1\\) or \\(3\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}}\n\\]"
