            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a **complete and rigorous** solution.  \nThe set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Non‑sunny lines are therefore vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).\n\n2. **Reduction to vertical non‑sunny lines.**  \n   For a fixed configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n}\\) we replace every non‑sunny line that is not vertical by a vertical line through a column that contains **at least as many points that are covered only by the replaced line**.  \n   Because a vertical line covers the whole column, the replacement never loses any point of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   Distinctness is preserved because we always choose a column not already used by another vertical line (there are at most \\(n-1\\) other lines).  \n   Repeating the replacement yields a configuration in which **all \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are vertical and occupy distinct columns**.  \n   Hence, if a value of \\(k\\) is impossible for such a reduced configuration, it is impossible for the original one.\n\n3. **The uncovered triangle.**  \n   After the reduction we may (and do) assume that the vertical lines occupy the leftmost \\(n-k\\) columns,\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\]\n   The remaining \\(k\\) right‑most columns,\n   \\[\n   x=n-k+1,\\dots ,x=n ,\n   \\]\n   are not covered by a vertical line.  \n   The points of \\(T_{n}\\) that lie in those columns form the right‑angled lattice triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta_{k}= \\Bigl\\{(x,y)\\;\\Big|\\;x=n-k+1,\\dots ,n,\\;\n                     1\\le y\\le k+1-(x-(n-k))\\Bigr\\},\n   \\]\n   which contains exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\Delta_{k}|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   lattice points.\n\n4. **How many points can a sunny line contain in \\(\\Delta_{k}\\)?**  \n\n   **Lemma 1.**  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\n   \\[\n   |L\\cap\\Delta_{k}|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write the line as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   For an integer abscissa \\(x\\) the ordinate is uniquely determined; therefore a line can meet at most one point of \\(\\Delta_{k}\\) in each column.\n\n   *Case \\(|m|\\ge 2\\).*  \n   When we move from column \\(x\\) to the next column \\(x+1\\) the ordinate changes by at least \\(2\\) in absolute value, whereas the admissible upper bound in \\(\\Delta_{k}\\) drops by exactly \\(1\\). Consequently after at most \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) steps the ordinate becomes non‑positive and the line leaves the triangle. Hence the line meets at most \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) columns, i.e. points.\n\n   *Case \\(|m|=1\\).*  \n   The only forbidden slope is \\(-1\\); the remaining possibilities are \\(m=1\\) and \\(m=-1\\) is excluded.  \n   For \\(m=1\\) we have \\(y=x+b\\). The condition \\(y\\le k+1-x\\) becomes \\(2x\\le k+1-b\\); thus \\(x\\) can take at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-b}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) integer values, which is never larger than \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\).  \n   The case \\(m=-1\\) does not occur because the line would be non‑sunny.\n\n   *Case \\(-1<m<0\\) or \\(0<m<1\\).*  \n   Write \\(m=-p/q\\) (or \\(m=p/q\\)) in lowest terms; then \\(q\\ge2\\).  \n   If the line contains two integer points \\((x_{1},y_{1})\\) and \\((x_{2},y_{2})\\) with \\(x_{2}>x_{1}\\), the difference in abscissas is a multiple of \\(q\\). Hence among any \\(q\\) consecutive columns the line can contain at most one lattice point. Because \\(q\\ge2\\), the line meets at most \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) columns of \\(\\Delta_{k}\\). ∎\n\n5. **Counting argument.**  \n   The \\(k\\) sunny lines together can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(\\Delta_{k}\\).  \n   By (1) we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Even \\(k\\).*  \n   Put \\(k=2t\\;(t\\ge1)\\). Then the left–hand side of (4) equals \\(2t^{2}\\) whereas the right–hand side equals \\(t(2t+1)=2t^{2}+t\\). Inequality (4) fails.  \n   Hence **no even \\(k\\ge2\\) is possible**.\n\n   *Odd \\(k\\).*  \n   Write \\(k=2t+1\\;(t\\ge0)\\). Then (3) equals \\((2t+1)(t+1)=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\); thus (4) holds with equality.  \n   Equality forces every sunny line to attain the maximal number \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil=t+1\\) of points in \\(\\Delta_{k}\\). Consequently each sunny line meets exactly \\(t+1\\) distinct columns of the triangle, and **every column of \\(\\Delta_{k}\\) is intersected by exactly as many lines as there are points in that column** (otherwise the total number of covered points would be smaller than \\(|\\Delta_{k}|\\)). In particular, column 1 (which contains \\(k\\) points) must contain a point of each sunny line; therefore each sunny line contains a point \\((n-k+1, y_i)\\) with the \\(y_i\\)’s forming a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\n\n   Consider now column 2