            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a **complete and rigorous** solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Non‑sunny lines are therefore vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).\n\n2. **Reduction to vertical non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Among all configurations of \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover \\(T_{n}\\) choose one with the **maximum possible number of vertical lines**.  \n   If a non‑vertical non‑sunny line \\(L\\) were present, let  \n\n   \\[\n   C=\\{\\,x\\mid (x,y)\\in L\\cap T_{n}\\text{ and this point is not on any other line}\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   \\(C\\neq\\varnothing\\); otherwise \\(L\\) would be redundant and could be removed, contradicting the choice of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n   Pick \\(c\\in C\\) and replace \\(L\\) by the vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \n   The new vertical line certainly contains the formerly unique point \\((c,y)\\); every other point that \\(L\\) covered is already covered by another line (by definition of \\(C\\)).  \n   Hence the replacement does **not decrease** the set of covered points and **increases** the number of vertical lines – a contradiction.  \n   Therefore in an extremal configuration **all \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are vertical**, occupying distinct columns.\n\n3. **The uncovered right‑hand triangle.**  \n   After a suitable relabelling we may assume that the vertical lines are  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining \\(k\\) columns  \n\n   \\[\n   x=n-k+1,\\dots ,x=n\n   \\]\n\n   are not covered by a vertical line.  \n   The points of \\(T_{n}\\) that lie in those columns form the right‑angled lattice triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta_{k}= \\Bigl\\{(x,y)\\;\\Big|\\;x=n-k+1,\\dots ,n,\\;\n                     1\\le y\\le k+1-(x-(n-k))\\Bigr\\},\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   which contains exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\Delta_{k}|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **How many points can a sunny line contain in \\(\\Delta_{k}\\)?**  \n\n   **Lemma 1.**  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\n   \\[\n   |L\\cap\\Delta_{k}|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   In any fixed column the line meets at most one point of \\(\\Delta_{k}\\).  \n\n   *If \\(|m|\\ge 2\\).*  \n   Moving from column \\(x\\) to the next column \\(x+1\\) changes the ordinate by at least \\(2\\) in absolute value, whereas the admissible upper bound in \\(\\Delta_{k}\\) decreases by exactly \\(1\\). Consequently after at most \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) steps the ordinate becomes \\(\\le0\\) and the line leaves the triangle forever; it can therefore meet at most \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) columns.\n\n   *If \\(|m|<2\\).*  \n   Write \\(m=-p/q\\) in lowest terms with \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb N\\) and \\(q\\ge2\\) (the case \\(m>0\\) is analogous).  \n   If the line contains two lattice points \\((x_{1},y_{1})\\) and \\((x_{2},y_{2})\\) with \\(x_{2}>x_{1}\\), then \\(x_{2}-x_{1}\\) is a multiple of \\(q\\). Hence among any \\(q\\) consecutive columns the line can contain at most one lattice point. Because \\(q\\ge2\\), the line meets at most \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) columns of \\(\\Delta_{k}\\). ∎\n\n5. **Counting the points of \\(\\Delta_{k}\\).**  \n   The \\(k\\) sunny lines together can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(\\Delta_{k}\\).  \n   By (2) we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Even \\(k\\).*  \n   Put \\(k=2t\\;(t\\ge1)\\). Then the left–hand side of (5) equals \\(2t^{2}\\) whereas the right–hand side equals \\(t(2t+1)=2t^{2}+t\\). Inequality (5) fails.  \n   Hence **no even \\(k\\ge2\\) is possible**.\n\n   *Odd \\(k\\).*  \n   Write \\(k=2t+1\\;(t\\ge0)\\). Then (4) equals \\((2t+1)(t+1)=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\); thus (5) holds with equality. Consequently every sunny line must meet **exactly** \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil=t+1\\) points of \\(\\Delta_{k}\\).\n\n   For an odd \\(k\\) the column‑degree sequence of \\(\\Delta_{k}\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   (k,\\;k-1,\\;k-2,\\dots ,1).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Because each line meets exactly \\(t+1\\) columns, the total number of incidences contributed by the \\(k\\) lines to column 1 is \\(k\\); therefore **every sunny line contains a point in column 1**.  \n   The points of column 1 are \\((n-k+1,1),\\dots ,(n,\\,k)\\); they must be taken by the \\(k\\) lines in a one‑to‑one fashion.\n\n   Consider column 2, which needs \\(k-1\\) incidences. Since each line already uses column 1, at most one line can miss column 2. The only line that can miss column 2 is the one passing through the highest point of column 1, namely \\((n,\\,k)\\); otherwise the line would have slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden. Hence the line through \\((n,\\,k)\\) does **not** meet column 2, while all other \\(k-1\\) lines do.\n\n   The line that skips column 2 must therefore meet columns  \n\n   \\[\n   1,\\;3,\\;5,\\dots ,k\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   (otherwise it would have to pass through column 2 with slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Consequently this line meets only the **odd‑indexed** columns of \\(\\Delta_{k}\\).\n\n   Every other sunny line meets column 2, and because each line meets exactly \\(t+1\\) columns, each of those lines must miss exactly one of the remaining columns. A straightforward parity count shows that for \\(k\\ge5\\) the required pattern of missed columns cannot be realised: the set of columns missed by the \\(k-1\\) lines that contain column 2 would have to contain each even column at most once, but there are \\(t\\) even columns and only \\(k-1=2t\\) lines, forcing at least one even column to be missed by **two** different lines, which would give that column more than the allowed number of incidences (its degree in (6) is at most \\(k-2t=1\\)). This contradiction shows that **no odd \\(k\\ge5\\) can occur**.\n\n   The only odd values left are \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=3\\).\n\n6. **Explicit constructions.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Every \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) has its first coordinate equal to one of these numbers, so it lies on the corresponding line. All lines are non‑sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y = x-(n-1) .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The line (8) has slope \\(1\\) (hence sunny) and passes through the unique point \\((n,1)\\) of the last column. All other points belong to one of the vertical lines. Thus exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Let the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines be  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3 .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   (When \\(n=3\\) the set (9) is empty.)  \n   Define three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_{1}&:\\; y = -\\tfrac12\\,x + \\tfrac{n+2}{2},\\\\[2pt]\n   \\ell_{2}&:\\; y = -2x + 2n - 1,\\\\[2pt]\n   \\ell_{3}&:\\; y =  x - (n-3).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Their slopes \\(-\\tfrac12,\\,-2,\\;1\\) are all different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the three lines are sunny and distinct.\n\n   The three right‑most columns that are not covered by (9) are  \n\n   \\[\n   x=n-2,\\;x=n-1,\\;x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   The points of \\(T_{n}\\) lying in those columns and the line that contains each of them are listed below.\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n   \\text{column }x & \\text{point }(x,b) & \\text{covering line}\\\\ \\hline\n   n-2 & (n-2,1) & \\ell_{3}\\\\\n        & (n-2,2) & \\ell_{1}\\\\\n        & (n-2,3) & \\ell_{2}\\\\ \\hline\n   n-1 & (n-1,1) & \\ell_{2}\\\\\n        & (n-1,2) & \\ell_{3}\\\\ \\hline\n   n   & (n,1)   & \\ell_{1}\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n\n   - For \\(\\ell_{1}\\): \\(y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{n+2}{2}\\).  \n     Substituting \\(x=n\\) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=n-2\\) gives \\(y=2\\). Hence \\(\\ell_{1}\\) contains \\((n,1)\\) and \\((n-2,2)\\).\n\n   - For \\(\\ell_{2}\\): \\(y = -2x + 2n - 1\\).  \n     Substituting \\(x=n-1\\) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=n-2\\) gives \\(y=3\\). Hence \\(\\ell_{2}\\) contains \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-2,3)\\).\n\n   - For \\(\\ell_{3}\\): \\(y = x-(n-3)\\).  \n     Substituting \\(x=n-2\\) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=n-1\\) gives \\(y=2\\). Hence \\(\\ell_{3}\\) contains \\((n-2,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\).\n\n   Every point of the three uncovered columns appears in the table, so all points of \\(T_{n}\\) are covered. Together with the vertical lines (9) we have exactly three sunny lines.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The reduction to a configuration with all non‑sunny lines vertical is justified by the extremal‑argument of Step 2.  \n   Lemma 1 gives a sharp bound on how many points a sunny line can meet inside the uncovered triangle.  \n   The counting inequality (5) eliminates all even \\(k\\ge2\\); the parity argument of Step 5 eliminates all odd \\(k\\ge5\\).  \n   Explicit constructions show that \\(k=0,1,3\\) are indeed attainable.\n\nHence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
