aximum possible number of vertical lines**.  \n> If a non‑vertical non‑sunny line \\(L\\) were present, let  \n> \\[\n> C=\\{\\,x\\mid (x,y)\\in L\\cap T_{n}\\text{ and this point is not on any other line}\\,\\}.\n> \\]  \n> \\(C\\neq\\varnothing\\); otherwise \\(L\\) would be redundant and could be removed, contradicting the choice of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n> Pick \\(c\\in C\\) and replace \\(L\\) by the vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \n> The new vertical line certainly contains the formerly unique point \\((c,y)\\); every other point that \\(L\\) covered is already covered by another line (by definition of \\(C\\)).  \n> Hence the replacement does **not decrease** the set of covered points and **increases** the number of vertical lines – a contradiction.  \n> Therefore in an extremal configuration **all \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are vertical**, occupying distinct columns.”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The definition of \\(C\\) is sound; if \\(C\\) were empty, every point of \\(L\\cap T_n\\) would be covered by another line, making \\(L\\) removable without loss of coverage.  \n- Because the problem only requires *exactly* \\(n\\) lines, a configuration with a redundant line is still admissible; however the extremal choice (maximising vertical lines) guarantees that a redundant non‑vertical line cannot appear, otherwise we could replace it by a new vertical line and increase the number of vertical lines, contradicting maximality.  \n- The point \\((c,y)\\) is not on any other line, so no vertical line \\(x=c\\) exists already; the replacement yields a distinct line.  \n- The replacement preserves coverage of all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nThus the argument correctly shows that we may assume **all non‑sunny lines are vertical**. No error.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3 – The uncovered right‑hand triangle  \n\n> “After a suitable relabelling we may assume that the vertical lines are  \n> \\[\n> x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n> \\]  \n> The remaining \\(k\\) columns  \n> \\[\n> x=n-k+1,\\dots ,x=n\n> \\]  \n> are not covered by a vertical line.  \n> The points of \\(T_{n}\\) that lie in those columns form the right‑angled lattice triangle  \n> \\[\n> \\Delta_{k}= \\Bigl\\{(x,y)\\;\\Big|\\;x=n-k+1,\\dots ,n,\\;\n>                      1\\le y\\le k+1-(x-(n-k))\\Bigr\\},\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]  \n> which contains exactly  \n> \\[\n> |\\Delta_{k}|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n> \\tag{2}\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The description of the uncovered columns is correct.  \n- For a column \\(x=n-k+1+i\\) (\\(0\\le i\\le k-1\\)), the condition \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) gives \\(y\\le k-i\\); the formula in (1) is equivalent to this bound.  \n- Summing the column sizes \\(k,k-1,\\dots ,1\\) yields \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).  \n\nAll statements are accurate.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4 – Lemma 1  \n\n> “**Lemma 1.**  \n> Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n> \\[\n> |L\\cap\\Delta_{k}|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]  \n> *Proof.*  \n> Write \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n> In any fixed column the line meets at most one point of \\(\\Delta_{k}\\).  \n> \n> *If \\(|m|\\ge 2\\).*  \n> Moving from column \\(x\\) to the next column \\(x+1\\) changes the ordinate by at least \\(2\\) in absolute value, whereas the admissible upper bound in \\(\\Delta_{k}\\) decreases by exactly \\(1\\). Consequently after at most \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) steps the ordinate becomes \\(\\le0\\) and the line leaves the triangle forever; it can therefore meet at most \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) columns.\n> \n> *If \\(|m|<2\\).*  \n> Write \\(m=-p/q\\) in lowest terms with \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb N\\) and \\(q\\ge2\\) (the case \\(m>0\\) is analogous).  \n> If the line contains two lattice points \\((x_{1},y_{1})\\) and \\((x_{2},y_{2})\\) with \\(x_{2}>x_{1}\\), then \\(x_{2}-x_{1}\\) is a multiple of \\(q\\). Hence among any \\(q\\) consecutive columns the line can contain at most one lattice point. Because \\(q\\ge2\\), the line meets at most \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) columns of \\(\\Delta_{k}\\). ∎”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The observation that a non‑vertical line meets at most one point per column is correct.  \n- **Case \\(|m|\\ge2\\):** The argument that the ordinate changes by at least 2 while the maximal allowed ordinate drops by 1 is sound for integer‑valued points; after at most \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) steps the line’s ordinate falls outside the interval \\([1,\\,\\text{max}]\\). Hence the bound holds.  \n- **Case \\(|m|<2\\):** The proof correctly handles rational slopes with denominator \\(q\\ge2\\). However, it **does not address the possibility that the slope \\(m\\) is irrational**. For an irrational slope a line can contain at most one lattice point (otherwise the slope would be rational), which would also satisfy the bound \\(\\le\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\). The omission of this case leaves the argument incomplete.  \n\n**Finding:** *Justification Gap* – the proof of Lemma 1 fails to treat irrational slopes explicitly.\n\n---\n\n### Step 5 – Counting the points of \\(\\Delta_{k}\\)\n\n> “The \\(k\\) sunny lines together can cover at most  \n> \\[\n> k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]  \n> points of \\(\\Delta_{k}\\).  \n> By (2) we must have  \n> \\[\n> k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- Using Lemma 1, each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) points, so the total is bounded by (4).  \n- Since all points of \\(\\Delta_k\\) must be covered, inequality (5) follows directly.  \n- The algebraic simplification that even \\(k\\ge2\\) violate (5) and odd \\(k\\) satisfy it with equality is correct.  \n\nNo issues here.\n\n---\n\n### Step 5 – Elimination of even \\(k\\)\n\n> “*Even \\(k\\).*  \n> Put \\(k=2t\\;(t\\ge1)\\). Then the left–hand side of (5) equals \\(2t^{2}\\) whereas the right–hand side equals \\(t(2t+1)=2t^{2}+t\\). Inequality (5) fails.  \n> Hence **no even \\(k\\ge2\\) is possible**