k=0\\).* …  \n> *\\(k=1\\).* …  \n> *\\(k=3\\).* … (construction with \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\) and verification table).”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- For \\(k=0\\) the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) clearly cover every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n- For \\(k=1\\) the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line \\(y=x-(n-1)\\) (slope 1) cover the unique point \\((n,1)\\) left uncovered by the vertical lines. All conditions are satisfied.  \n- For \\(k=3\\) the construction is checked column‑by‑column in the provided table; substitution confirms that each listed point lies on the indicated line. The vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) cover all remaining points. Hence the construction works for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\nAll three constructions are correct.\n\n---\n\n### Step 7 – Conclusion  \n\n> “Hence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) … is \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).”\n\n**Verification:** The conclusion relies on the parity argument of Step 5 to rule out all odd \\(k\\ge5\\). Since that argument contains a **Critical Error**, the conclusion is not justified. The existence of configurations for \\(k=0,1,3\\) is established, but the impossibility of any other \\(k\\) remains unproven.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:**  \nThe solution correctly handles the reduction to vertical non‑sunny lines, the counting of points, and provides valid constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\). However:\n\n1. **Lemma 1** omits the case of irrational slopes (Justification Gap).  \n2. The **parity argument** that eliminates all odd \\(k\\ge5\\) is based on a false identification of a point and an incorrect claim about which line can miss column 2 (Critical Error).  \n\nBecause the impossibility of odd \\(k\\ge5\\) is not rigorously established, the answer \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\) is **not proved**. Therefore the solution is invalid."
